;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;
; File: mfunc20.mac
; Desc: M20 -- ATC Reset (the one source of truth for the reset action)
;
; Run by the VCP ATC RESET button and by system/plcmacro4.mac (wireless MPG
; macro button 4). Both are one-liners that call M20, so the logic lives here.
;
; Why a dummy tool at all: CNC12 skips an M6 for the tool its status window
; says is loaded, and that tool is read-only to us. After a hand-moved carousel
; a change back to the loaded tool is silently ignored -- no error, no motion --
; while the PLC's hand-move interlock keeps refusing the spindle. A change to a
; tool CNC12 does NOT think is loaded always runs: the carousel search proves
; the position, the interlock clears, and CNC12's end-of-M6 bookkeeping puts the
; previously loaded tool back in its own bin.
;
; Why TWO dummies: after one reset the loaded tool IS the dummy, so reusing it
; would be skipped and a second reset would do nothing (found on-machine
; 2026-09-09). 199 and 200 alternate, so a reset always works, every time.
;
; Setup: tools 199 and 200 both assigned the SAME bin in the Tool Library, with
; H and D offsets 0. Sharing a bin is explicitly allowed by CNC12 and keeps the
; carousel parking in the same place whichever dummy is used. Follow a reset
; with a real T## M6.
;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;

;Skip if graphing or searching
IF #4201 || #4202 THEN GOTO 1000

; #4203 = the tool CNC12 believes is in the spindle. Pick the dummy that is NOT
; it, so the change can never be skipped.
IF #4203 == 200 THEN GOTO 100

T200 M6         ; normal case: 200 is not loaded, so this change runs
GOTO 1000

N100
T199 M6         ; 200 is loaded (a previous reset), so use the other dummy

N1000           ; end of program
